(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e1.41532ffbdb.js"]={"1":[{"id":"sdl01_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Distinguishing Between Random and Nonrandom Sampling","batch":1,"stem":"Which feature defines probability sampling?","choices":{"A":"Subjects are chosen for their availability and accessibility","B":"Subjects are hand-picked by the researcher for relevant expertise","C":"Fixed proportions of visible subgroups are filled deliberately","D":"Each element has an equal and known chance of being selected","E":"Participants refer other eligible people from their networks"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Random (probability) sampling is built on the theory of probability: each element in the population has an equal, known chance of being selected, which makes the sample likely to be representative. Selection by availability is convenience sampling, hand-picking for expertise is judgmental or expert sampling, filling fixed proportions of visible subgroups is quota sampling, and peer referral is snowball sampling. All four are nonrandom methods. Strictly, equal chance describes simple random sampling; every random design gives each element a known chance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid defines selection bias as nonrandom sampling or treatment allocation, most commonly a sampling bias, with convenience sampling as its example. COMLEX: random selection is what allows sample statistics to be generalized to the target population.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — nonrandom selection opens the door to selection bias, a systematic difference between the people selected and those who are not","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Distinguishing Between Random and Nonrandom Sampling","batch":1,"stem":"A reviewer states that a study sample cannot be used to statistically extrapolate its findings to the larger population. Which property of the sampling process best accounts for that limitation?","choices":{"A":"Strata were sampled in proportion to their size","B":"Selection did not follow the theory of probability","C":"A complete sampling frame was used for selection","D":"Clusters were chosen randomly from the population","E":"Each unit had an equal chance of being drawn"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Nonrandom (nonprobability) sampling does not follow the theory of probability; subjects are chosen for practical reasons or researcher judgment, so the sample cannot be used to statistically extrapolate inferences to the population. Proportionate stratified sampling, selection from a complete sampling frame, random selection of clusters, and equal selection chance are all features of random sampling, which supports inference.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid defines selection bias as nonrandom sampling or treatment allocation such that the study population is not representative of the target population. COMLEX: findings from a nonrepresentative sample should not be generalized to a clinic population without caution.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — nonrandom selection opens the door to selection bias, a systematic difference between the people selected and those who are not","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q03","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — The Importance of Random Sampling for a Representative Sample","batch":1,"stem":"Why do the statistical methods used to calculate confidence intervals and perform hypothesis tests depend on how the sample was obtained?","choices":{"A":"They require that subjects be chosen by expert judgment","B":"They automatically correct for bias in subject selection","C":"Their mathematical models presume random selection of subjects","D":"They require that each stratum contribute equal numbers","E":"They apply exclusively to homogeneous target populations"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The mathematical models behind confidence intervals, hypothesis testing, and generalization from sample to population presume that a random sample was used; without random selection these tools lose their mathematical validity. Statistical methods do not correct for biased selection, they do not require expert judgment, they are not restricted to homogeneous populations, and equal allocation is one optional form of stratified sampling rather than a requirement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid defines selection bias as nonrandom sampling such that the study population is not representative of the target population. COMLEX: a convenience sample from a single clinic can over-represent one subgroup of a heterogeneous population.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 -> Obj 1 — a sample statistic serves as an estimate of the population parameter when the sample represents the population","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q04","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Subtypes of Random and Nonrandom Sampling","batch":1,"stem":"A heterogeneous population is divided into relatively homogeneous subgroups by profession, and a random sample is then drawn from within each subgroup. Which sampling method is described?","choices":{"A":"Simple random sampling","B":"Stratified random sampling","C":"Systematic random sampling","D":"Multistage cluster sampling","E":"Consecutive sampling"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Stratified random sampling divides a heterogeneous population into homogeneous subgroups called strata and samples randomly within each, so every subgroup is represented; allocation can be proportionate, equal, or disproportionate. Simple random sampling draws from the whole frame without strata, systematic sampling takes every kth unit after a random start, cluster sampling selects natural groupings and studies everyone in them, and consecutive sampling is a nonrandom method that enrolls every eligible patient over a time period.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid defines selection bias as nonrandom sampling that leaves the study population unrepresentative; note that stratified sampling is a design choice, distinct from the stratified analysis First Aid uses to separate confounding from effect modification. COMLEX: disproportionate stratified sampling over-samples rare subgroups of special interest.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 3 — how subjects are sampled shapes whether findings can be generalized to other populations","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q05","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Subtypes of Random and Nonrandom Sampling","batch":1,"stem":"A target population is so large and geographically spread out that no complete list of individuals can be obtained. Researchers randomly select natural groupings such as schools and households and then study every individual within the chosen groupings. Which sampling method is this?","choices":{"A":"Quota sampling","B":"Stratified sampling","C":"Systematic sampling","D":"Cluster sampling","E":"Simple random sampling"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cluster sampling is used when the population is very large or geographically vast and a complete sampling frame is impossible; natural groupings are sampled at random and all individuals within chosen clusters are studied, in single-stage, double-stage, or multistage designs. Quota sampling is nonrandom, stratified sampling samples within every stratum rather than taking whole groups, and both systematic and simple random sampling need a complete list of units.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid treats sampling that leaves the study population unrepresentative of the target population as selection bias. COMLEX: community surveys of households or schools commonly use multistage cluster designs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 3 — how subjects are sampled shapes whether findings can be generalized to other populations","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Distinguishing Between Random and Nonrandom Sampling","batch":2,"stem":"A family medicine resident wants to estimate the prevalence of hypertension among all adults living in her county. Over two weeks she enrolls the first 200 adults who agree to have their blood pressure measured at a downtown free clinic where she works. She plans to report the clinic prevalence as the county prevalence. Which statement best describes her sample?","choices":{"A":"It is a simple random sample of adults in the county","B":"It is a systematic sample with a fixed sampling interval","C":"It is a cluster sample drawn from county neighborhoods","D":"It is a convenience sample unsuited to county-wide inference","E":"It is a stratified sample that represents each county age group"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Enrolling readily available, willing adults at one clinic is convenience sampling, a nonrandom method; because county residents did not have an equal chance of selection, the clinic prevalence cannot be statistically extrapolated to the county. There was no random selection from a county list, no random choice of neighborhoods, no fixed interval after a random start, and no division into strata.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid names convenience sampling, with patients enrolled on the basis of ease of contact, as its example of selection bias. COMLEX: a free clinic population can differ from the general county population in access to care and disease burden.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — selection bias from a lack of comparability between those studied and those not studied","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q07","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — The Importance of Random Sampling for a Representative Sample","batch":2,"stem":"A city health department wants to estimate vaccine acceptance among its residents, a population that is highly diverse in income, age, and ethnicity. Interviewers survey 400 shoppers at a single upscale mall on weekday mornings. The reported acceptance is far higher than the rate later found in a city-wide random survey. Which explanation best accounts for the discrepancy?","choices":{"A":"Convenient selection over-represented one population subgroup","B":"Excessive homogeneity of the city population being studied","C":"Use of strata that were proportionate to population size","D":"Random sampling variation that would vanish with a bigger sample","E":"Confounding by age between attitude and vaccination status"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Choosing subjects for convenience introduces systematic bias, which is most damaging in heterogeneous populations because the convenient sample can over-represent a particular subgroup and give misleading conclusions. Random variation does not push results consistently in one direction, the population was heterogeneous rather than homogeneous, no strata were used, and the survey measured a single attitude rather than an exposure-outcome association that a confounder could distort.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in First Aid, selection bias leaves the study population unrepresentative of the target population and is reduced by randomization. COMLEX: public health surveys use random sampling so that prevalence estimates apply to the whole community.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — bias distorts results in one direction, whereas chance varies in both directions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q08","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — The Importance of Random Sampling for a Representative Sample","batch":2,"stem":"An investigator has a complete and current list of all 1,200 residents of an assisted-living network. She wants the mean systolic blood pressure of her sample to closely mirror the true mean of the whole network. Which step in choosing her sample most directly supports this goal?","choices":{"A":"Selecting residents with a random number generator","B":"Enrolling the residents who volunteer at the first visit","C":"Choosing residents the nursing staff judge most reliable","D":"Asking enrolled residents to refer their neighbors","E":"Enrolling residents seen in the morning clinic each day"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Random selection from a complete sampling frame gives every resident an equal chance of inclusion, and this objectivity lets sample statistics such as the mean closely mirror the population parameter; simple random sampling suits a relatively small population with a complete list. Enrolling volunteers or morning-clinic patients is convenience sampling, staff selection is judgmental sampling, and referral is snowball sampling, all of which can introduce systematic bias.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid defines selection bias as nonrandom sampling such that the study population is not representative of the target population, with convenience sampling as its example. COMLEX: volunteers often differ systematically in health from people who do not volunteer.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 1 — sample statistics used as estimates of population parameters","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Subtypes of Random and Nonrandom Sampling","batch":2,"stem":"A qualitative research team wants to describe barriers to care among people who inject drugs in a large city. No list of this population exists, and many members avoid health services. The team recruits 12 eligible participants through a harm-reduction program, interviews them, and then asks each one to put the team in touch with other eligible people they know. Which sampling technique is being used?","choices":{"A":"Cluster sampling","B":"Quota sampling","C":"Expert sampling","D":"Consecutive sampling","E":"Snowball sampling"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Snowball sampling starts with a small group that has the desired characteristics and asks them to refer other eligible people from their networks, so the sample grows; it is particularly useful for hard-to-reach or marginalized groups such as people who use drugs. Cluster sampling randomly selects natural groupings, quota sampling fills fixed proportions of visible characteristics, expert sampling targets recognized experts, and consecutive sampling enrolls every eligible patient over a time period.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid treats any nonrandom sampling that leaves the study population unrepresentative as selection bias. COMLEX: network-based recruitment is common in studies of stigmatized conditions and should be described as nonrandom when results are reported.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — internal validity versus external validity and generalizability","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q10","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Subtypes of Random and Nonrandom Sampling","batch":2,"stem":"An emergency department study of the causes of fever in adults enrolls every patient who meets the inclusion criteria from early December through February, until 300 patients are reached. A reviewer notes that influenza accounts for an unexpectedly large share of the final diagnoses compared with year-round data. Which statement best describes this design and its weakness?","choices":{"A":"Simple random sampling distorted by measurement bias","B":"Systematic sampling distorted by a periodic list order","C":"Cluster sampling distorted by unequal neighborhood sizes","D":"Snowball sampling distorted by network referrals","E":"Consecutive sampling vulnerable to a seasonal trend"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Enrolling every consecutive eligible patient over a set timeframe until the target size is reached is consecutive sampling, a superior nonrandom approach that restricts sampling bias, but investigators must watch for temporal or seasonal trends such as a winter influenza peak. Patients were not drawn randomly from a list, no every-kth interval was used, there were no referrals, and no geographic groupings were sampled.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists selection bias as arising when the study population is not representative of the target population. COMLEX: seasonal patterns in respiratory illness can distort a study enrolled during a single season.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — external validity and whether findings apply to other settings","isHighYield":false}],"2":[{"id":"sdl02_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Research Hypotheses vs. Statistical Hypotheses","batch":1,"stem":"Clinicians noticed that patients taking the antihypertensive minoxidil developed excessive hair growth, and they proposed that the drug might treat alopecia. How is this proposal best classified?","choices":{"A":"A null hypothesis","B":"A research hypothesis","C":"A significance level","D":"A confounder","E":"A point estimate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A research hypothesis is the clinical supposition that motivates a study and typically arises from careful clinical observation, as when minoxidil-associated hair growth suggested a treatment for alopecia. Statistical hypotheses, the null and the alternative, are formal assumptions about a population parameter written so they can be tested statistically, and they are formulated from the research hypothesis. A confounding variable, a significance level, and a point estimate are not hypotheses.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid defines the null hypothesis as no difference or association and the alternative as at least one difference or relationship. COMLEX: careful observation of unexpected drug effects, such as amantadine reducing tremor, generates new research hypotheses.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 2 — statistical hypothesis tests presume a random sample drawn from the target population","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Research Hypotheses vs. Statistical Hypotheses","batch":1,"stem":"Investigators design a trial comparing survival with two antiarrhythmic drugs, A and B. Which statement is an example of the null hypothesis for this trial?","choices":{"A":"Drug A yields longer survival than drug B in the population","B":"The trial has an 80 percent probability of detecting a benefit","C":"The observed survival difference has a p-value below 0.05","D":"The true difference in survival between the drugs is zero","E":"Survival differs between the drugs by a clinically useful margin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The null hypothesis is formulated to be tested and either rejected or not rejected, and it generally states that there is no difference between treatments, here that the true survival difference is zero. A statement that one drug is better is an alternative hypothesis, a p-value below 0.05 is a test result, an 80 percent probability of detecting a benefit describes power, and a clinically useful margin concerns clinical significance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid writes the null hypothesis as H0, no difference or association in the population, and the alternative as H1. COMLEX: in most clinical research the alternative hypothesis and the research hypothesis are the same.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 2 — statistical hypothesis tests presume a random sample drawn from the target population","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q03","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Importance and Use of p-Values in Hypothesis Testing","batch":1,"stem":"A trial comparing two treatments reports p = 0.75 for the difference in its primary outcome. Which conclusion is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"There is a 75 percent chance the treatment is effective","B":"The null hypothesis has been proven false by the trial","C":"The difference is statistically significant at alpha 0.05","D":"A clinically important difference has been ruled out","E":"The evidence is too weak to reject the null hypothesis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A p-value of 0.75 is far above the conventional 0.05 cutoff, so the observed difference could easily have arisen by chance and the evidence is too weak to reject the null hypothesis. It does not give the probability that a treatment works, it does not disprove the null hypothesis, and it is not significant. Because a p-value cannot convey the magnitude of a difference, it also cannot by itself exclude a clinically important effect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid defines the P value as the probability of obtaining results at least as extreme as those observed, assuming H0 is correct, with 0.05 commonly accepted as the cutoff. COMLEX: a nonsignificant result is not proof that two treatments are equivalent.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 -> Obj 3 — confidence intervals replace the yes-or-no p-value decision with a continuum of plausible effects","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q04","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Type I Error, Type II Error, and Power","batch":1,"stem":"Which statement defines a type II error?","choices":{"A":"Rejecting a null hypothesis that is actually false","B":"Rejecting a null hypothesis that is actually true","C":"Failing to reject a null hypothesis that is actually false","D":"Failing to reject a null hypothesis that is actually true","E":"Overestimating a treatment effect because of systematic bias"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A type II error is a false-negative conclusion: the investigator fails to reject a null hypothesis that is not true, concluding there is no difference when one exists; its probability is beta. Rejecting a true null hypothesis is a type I error, a false positive with probability alpha. Rejecting a false null and failing to reject a true null are both correct conclusions, and overestimation from systematic error is bias rather than a hypothesis-testing error.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid calls the type II error a false negative, the second time the boy cries wolf and the town believes there is no wolf, and power equals 1 minus beta. COMLEX: an underpowered trial can miss a beneficial therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 — chance is random error, and a larger sample reduces its influence","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q05","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Statistical Significance vs. Clinical Significance","batch":1,"stem":"A randomized controlled trial of donepezil versus placebo in Alzheimer disease found p < 0.0001 for cognitive ratings, but the mean difference was 0.8 points on the 30-point MMSE and 1 point on the 60-point BADLS. How is this result best characterized?","choices":{"A":"High clinical importance obscured by a type II error","B":"A false-positive conclusion caused by setting alpha above 0.05","C":"A nonsignificant result that needs a larger trial","D":"Strong statistical significance of little clinical value","E":"A result whose p-value conveys its clinical magnitude"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Statistical significance shows how confident we are that a difference exists but not how large or clinically important it is; a difference of 0.8 points on a 30-point scale is clinically negligible despite p < 0.0001. The result is significant, so neither a type II error nor a nonsignificant result applies, nothing suggests alpha was set above 0.05, and a p-value cannot convey the magnitude of an effect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid states that statistical significance is not clinical significance, using a statistically significant 1 mg/dL drop in blood sugar as an example of limited clinical impact. COMLEX: judge an effect by its size and its meaning to the patient, not by its p-value.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 -> Obj 3 — a confidence interval compared with a clinical benefit threshold separates definitive from non-definitive results","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Importance and Use of p-Values in Hypothesis Testing","batch":2,"stem":"Two randomized trials compare the same new antihypertensive with placebo, using the same endpoint of mean reduction in systolic blood pressure. Trial 1 enrolls 300 patients and reports p = 0.04; Trial 2 enrolls 3,000 patients and reports p = 0.008 for the difference between drug and placebo. Which statement about these results is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Trial 1 failed to reach statistical significance","B":"Trial 2 shows a larger and more clinically important effect","C":"Chance is less likely to explain the result of Trial 2","D":"Trial 1 most likely represents a type II error","E":"Trial 2 proves that the alternative hypothesis is true"},"correct":"C","explanation":"As the p-value gets smaller, the probability that the observed difference is due to chance decreases, so p = 0.008 makes chance a less likely explanation than p = 0.04; both results are below 0.05 and are significant. Because Trial 2 is ten times larger, its smaller p-value can reflect its size rather than a bigger effect, and p-values cannot convey magnitude or clinical importance. A type II error is a failure to reject a false null hypothesis, which did not occur in either trial because both rejected it, and hypothesis testing rejects the null rather than proving the alternative.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes that H1 can never be proven; H0 can only be rejected as very unlikely. COMLEX: report the size of a blood pressure reduction alongside its p-value when counseling patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 4 — statistical significance versus clinical significance","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q07","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Importance and Use of p-Values in Hypothesis Testing","batch":2,"stem":"A conference abstract states that a new analgesic \"significantly reduced postoperative pain scores compared with placebo, p = 0.03\" and reports nothing else about the result. A faculty member says the abstract leaves out what matters most for deciding whether to use the drug. Which additional information is most needed?","choices":{"A":"Whether the null hypothesis was rejected at alpha 0.05","B":"A recalculated p-value reported to four decimal places","C":"The power the trial was originally designed to have","D":"The size and clinical importance of the difference","E":"The significance level used to define the threshold"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The p-value of 0.03 already tells the reader that the result crossed the conventional 0.05 threshold and that the null hypothesis was rejected, so a more precise p-value, the significance level, or the planned power adds little. What a p-value cannot convey is the actual magnitude or clinical importance of the difference, and it forces a yes-or-no decision on what is really a continuum of effectiveness; a difference in pain scores can be significant yet too small to matter to patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid states that statistical significance does not equal clinical significance; clinical significance measures the effect on treatment outcomes. COMLEX: ask how large the effect was before changing a pain regimen.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 3 — confidence intervals present treatment effects as a continuum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Type I Error, Type II Error, and Power","batch":2,"stem":"Several small early randomized trials of thrombolytic therapy for acute myocardial infarction each showed clinically promising reductions in mortality, yet each reported p > 0.05. When the results of these trials were later pooled in a meta-analysis, the benefit was clearly demonstrated both clinically and statistically. Which best explains the nonsignificant results of the individual trials?","choices":{"A":"Type I errors from inflated significance levels","B":"Type II errors from inadequate study power","C":"A true absence of any treatment effect","D":"Confounding by severity of the infarction","E":"Systematic bias favoring the control treatment"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Each early trial was too small, giving inadequate power and making it highly susceptible to a type II error that obscured a real, clinically important benefit; pooling the trials increased the sample size and revealed the effect. A type I error is a false positive, which cannot explain a failure to find a difference. The meta-analysis showed that the effect was real, and nothing in the trials points to bias or confounding as the cause of the nonsignificant results.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists increased sample size, effect size, measurement precision, and alpha as factors that increase power, and states that meta-analysis improves power and generalizability. COMLEX: a negative small trial does not rule out a benefit.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 3 — non-definitive negative trials and the need for larger studies","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Type I Error, Type II Error, and Power","batch":2,"stem":"Investigators planning a randomized trial of a new cardiology drug set alpha at 0.05 and beta at 0.20 before calculating the required sample size. The calculation calls for 600 patients, but they decide to enroll 40 patients because of cost. If the drug truly works as well as they specified, what is the main danger of proceeding with 40 patients?","choices":{"A":"Selection bias created by the smaller enrollment","B":"Missing a real benefit of the new medication","C":"A false-positive rate greater than 5 percent","D":"Confounding introduced by the reduced sample size","E":"Measurement bias from fewer outcome assessments"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Alpha of 0.05 and beta of 0.20 give a planned power of 80 percent only at the calculated sample size; a much smaller sample has inadequate power and is highly susceptible to a type II error that misses a true effect. Alpha is chosen by the investigators, so the false-positive rate stays at 5 percent whatever the enrollment, and because the drug truly works a false positive is not the danger here. Sample size governs chance, not systematic bias, so selection bias, confounding, and measurement bias do not arise from enrolling fewer patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes that if you increase sample size you increase power, because there is power in numbers. COMLEX: underpowered trials expose patients to research risk without a realistic chance of answering the question.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — chance can be reduced by a larger sample, whereas bias cannot","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Statistical Significance vs. Clinical Significance","batch":2,"stem":"A trial of a new glucose-lowering drug in 20,000 adults with type 2 diabetes finds that fasting glucose fell 1 mg/dL more with the drug than with placebo, p < 0.001. Reviewers point out that the p-value forces a yes-or-no verdict and says nothing about whether the effect matters to patients. Which reporting approach best conveys both the magnitude and the precision of this effect?","choices":{"A":"A confidence interval around the mean difference","B":"A larger sample to reach stronger significance","C":"A statement rejecting the null hypothesis","D":"A smaller p-value threshold such as 0.001","E":"Reporting the study power of 80 percent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Confidence intervals give the single value most likely to represent the true difference together with a plausible range, expressing the effect and its precision as a continuum rather than a yes-or-no decision. A stricter p-value threshold, a larger sample, or a statement that the null hypothesis was rejected still describe only whether a difference exists, and planned power describes the design rather than the size of the observed effect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid uses a statistically significant 1 mg/dL drop in blood sugar as its example of a result with limited clinical significance. COMLEX: very large trials can make trivial differences statistically significant.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — point estimates versus interval estimates","isHighYield":true}],"3":[{"id":"sdl03_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Definition and Rationale for Estimation in Health Sciences Research","batch":1,"stem":"In health sciences research, what is estimation?","choices":{"A":"Using a sample statistic to approximate a population parameter","B":"Deciding whether to reject a null hypothesis at alpha 0.05","C":"Calculating the probability that a difference is due to chance","D":"Measuring a characteristic in every member of the target population","E":"Assigning subjects to groups so known variables are balanced"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Estimation is the process of calculating a sample statistic, such as a sample mean or proportion, to serve as an approximate value of the corresponding unknown population parameter. Deciding whether to reject the null hypothesis and calculating the role of chance are parts of hypothesis testing, measuring every member of the population is a census and is what estimation avoids, and balancing groups is the purpose of randomization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid defines the confidence interval as the range within which the true population mean is expected to fall with a specified probability. COMLEX: clinical audit often estimates a yearly figure from a random sample of records.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 2 — sample statistics mirror population parameters when the sample is drawn at random","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl03_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Definition and Rationale for Estimation in Health Sciences Research","batch":1,"stem":"Why do health sciences researchers generally study a sample to learn about a target population rather than conducting a census of the entire population?","choices":{"A":"Population parameters change with each new sample drawn","B":"Measuring every person is too costly and time-consuming","C":"Population data cannot be summarized by a mean or proportion","D":"Statistical tests are invalid when the full population is known","E":"Samples always reproduce the population parameter exactly"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Target populations are typically very large, so measuring every individual is practically impossible because of financial, temporal, and logistical constraints; researchers therefore draw a representative sample and use its statistics to estimate the population parameters. A population parameter is constant, whereas sample statistics vary from sample to sample and rarely match the parameter exactly. Population data can be summarized by means and proportions, and knowing the full population would make estimation unnecessary rather than invalid.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid defines selection bias as nonrandom sampling that leaves the study population unrepresentative of the target population, which is why estimates must come from a representative sample. COMLEX: a pain clinic can estimate a full year of patient characteristics from patients seen on randomly selected days.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 2 — sample statistics mirror population parameters when the sample is drawn at random","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl03_q03","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Point Estimates vs. Interval Estimates","batch":1,"stem":"A random sample of 50 adults has a mean systolic blood pressure of 120 mmHg, and the investigator uses this single number as the best guess for the population. What is the 120 mmHg value an example of?","choices":{"A":"A confidence interval","B":"A population parameter","C":"A significance level","D":"A sampling frame","E":"A point estimate"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A point estimate is a single numerical value calculated from sample data to estimate a population parameter, such as a sample mean of 120 mmHg. A confidence interval is a range of values rather than a single number, the population parameter is the unknown true value being estimated, a sampling frame is the list of units from which a sample is drawn, and a significance level is the alpha threshold for hypothesis testing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid builds the CI for a sample mean around the point estimate as the mean plus or minus Z times the standard error, with Z = 1.96 for a 95% CI. COMLEX: report a sample mean with its confidence interval so its precision is visible.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 — sampling error is one of the sources of random error that an interval estimate accounts for","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Point Estimates vs. Interval Estimates","batch":1,"stem":"What is the main limitation of a point estimate when it is reported by itself?","choices":{"A":"It cannot be computed for a sample proportion","B":"It systematically overestimates the population value","C":"It requires measurement of the entire population","D":"It does not show how close it lies to the truth","E":"It is valid exclusively for very large samples"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Because of natural sampling variation, a point estimate rarely coincides exactly with the true population parameter, and on its own it gives no information about how close it is to the truth; an interval estimate adds that information. A point estimate does not systematically overestimate, it is calculated from a sample rather than the whole population, it can be a sample proportion as well as a mean, and it can be calculated for samples of any size.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes that the CI narrows as sample size increases. COMLEX: a single percentage from a small study should be read with its interval before it guides practice.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 — sampling error is one of the sources of random error that an interval estimate accounts for","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q05","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Practical Applications of Confidence Intervals","batch":1,"stem":"Compared with relying on a p-value cutoff such as p < 0.05, what is the main advantage of a confidence interval for interpreting a trial result?","choices":{"A":"It eliminates the influence of systematic bias","B":"It shows the effect as a range of plausible values","C":"It guarantees the true value lies in the range","D":"It removes the need for a random sample","E":"It converts the result into a yes-or-no decision"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hypothesis testing makes a dichotomous yes-or-no decision from an arbitrary p-value cutoff, whereas a confidence interval presents the treatment effect as a continuum of plausible values for the true effect. A confidence interval accounts for sampling error but not for systematic bias, it still assumes a random sample, and a 95% interval carries a specified degree of certainty rather than a guarantee.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid states that H0 is rejected when the 95% CI for a mean difference excludes 0, or when the 95% CI for an odds ratio or relative risk excludes 1. COMLEX: read the interval of a trial result, not just whether it was significant.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 -> Obj 3 and 4 — wide intervals in small trials reflect inadequate power, and clinical importance is judged by effect size","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Point Estimates vs. Interval Estimates","batch":2,"stem":"A consultant at a pain clinic wants to know the mean age of all patients seen during the past year. Instead of searching thousands of records, she randomly selects patients from 30 clinic days. Their mean age is 58 years, and she reports that the true mean age of the year's patients lies between 55 and 61 years with 95 percent confidence. Which statement about the range of 55 to 61 years is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"It is the exact mean age of all patients seen that year","B":"It is a point estimate of the population parameter","C":"It accounts for sampling error around the sample mean","D":"It describes the range of ages of the individual patients","E":"It removes the need to sample the patients at random"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The 55-61 year range is an interval estimate, or confidence interval, constructed around the point estimate of 58 years to account for the sampling error inherent in studying a subset of the population. It gives a plausible range for the unknown true mean rather than the exact mean, it is not a single-value point estimate, it describes where the population mean is likely to lie rather than the spread of individual ages, and its validity depends on random sampling.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid gives the CI for a sample mean as the mean plus or minus Z times the standard error, so a larger standard error widens the interval. COMLEX: distinguish the spread of individual values from the precision of a mean.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — random sampling and a representative sample","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Practical Applications of Confidence Intervals","batch":2,"stem":"In a secondary-prevention trial in patients with a prior ischemic stroke, 9% of patients receiving acetylsalicylic acid had a recurrent stroke, compared with 7% of patients receiving placebo. The difference was not statistically significant. The 95% confidence interval for the difference ranged from 3% fewer strokes with acetylsalicylic acid to 7% more strokes with acetylsalicylic acid. Which conclusion is best supported?","choices":{"A":"The drug definitively has no clinically useful benefit","B":"The drug is proven to raise stroke risk by 2 percent","C":"A benefit of up to 3 percent with the drug remains plausible","D":"The trial was large enough to give a precise answer","E":"Placebo is proven better than the drug at preventing stroke"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The point estimate of 2% more strokes superficially suggests harm, but the confidence interval extends to 3% fewer strokes with the drug, so preventing stroke in up to 3% of patients is still statistically plausible and a much larger trial is needed. The interval includes zero, meaning no difference, so neither harm nor placebo superiority is proven, a benefit cannot be ruled out, and an interval spanning 10 percentage points is too wide to be called precise.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid states that H0 is not rejected when the 95% CI for a mean difference includes 0. COMLEX: a \"negative\" trial with a wide interval is not evidence that a therapy is useless.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — type II error and inadequate power","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Practical Applications of Confidence Intervals","batch":2,"stem":"Two research groups test the same lipid-lowering drug against placebo with identical methods, endpoints, and analyses. Group X enrolls 200 patients and Group Y enrolls 2,000 patients, and both obtain the same point estimate for the reduction in LDL cholesterol. Which difference between their 95% confidence intervals is expected?","choices":{"A":"Both groups will yield intervals of identical width","B":"Group X will yield the more precise of the two intervals","C":"Group X will yield an interval at a higher confidence level","D":"Group Y will yield an interval centered on a larger effect","E":"Group Y will yield a narrower and more precise interval"},"correct":"E","explanation":"There is a direct relationship between sample size and confidence interval width: the larger the sample, the narrower and more precise the interval, so Group Y with ten times as many patients will have the narrower interval. With the same point estimate both intervals are centered in the same place, the smaller study gives a wider and less precise interval, and the confidence level, 95%, is chosen by the investigators rather than set by sample size.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid states that as sample size increases, the CI narrows. COMLEX: interval width tells you whether a study was large enough to give a definitive answer.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — sample size, power, and type II error","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Practical Applications of Confidence Intervals","batch":2,"stem":"Before a trial of a new anticoagulant begins, the investigators decide that an absolute risk reduction of at least 5 percentage points is required for the drug to be clinically useful. The trial finds an absolute risk reduction of 9 percentage points with a 95% confidence interval of 6 to 12 percentage points. How should this outcome be classified?","choices":{"A":"Non-definitive positive","B":"Definitive negative","C":"Definitive positive","D":"Non-definitive negative","E":"Statistically nonsignificant"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The entire 95% confidence interval, from 6 to 12 percentage points, lies above the predetermined clinical benefit threshold of 5, so the trial is definitive positive and a true, clinically important advantage can be accepted with high confidence. Non-definitive positive would require the lower limit to fall below the threshold, definitive negative requires the whole interval to lie below the threshold, non-definitive negative fails to reject the null hypothesis while its interval still reaches above the threshold, and an interval that excludes zero is statistically significant.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid states that H0 is rejected when the 95% CI for a mean difference excludes 0. COMLEX: comparing the whole interval with a clinically meaningful threshold is how trial results are translated into practice decisions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 4 — statistical significance versus clinical significance","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q10","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Practical Applications of Confidence Intervals","batch":2,"stem":"Investigators set a clinical benefit threshold of a 5 percentage-point absolute risk reduction for a new antiplatelet drug. The trial reports an absolute risk reduction of 2 percentage points, with a 95% confidence interval of -4 to 8 percentage points, and the null hypothesis is not rejected. How should this outcome be classified?","choices":{"A":"Definitive negative","B":"Definitive positive","C":"Non-definitive positive","D":"Non-definitive negative","E":"Statistically significant benefit"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The interval includes zero, so the trial fails to reject the null hypothesis, but its upper limit of 8 percentage points crosses above the clinical benefit threshold of 5, so a clinically important benefit is still possible; this is a non-definitive negative result, and a larger trial is needed before a final conclusion. Negative here means the trial failed to reject the null hypothesis; the point estimate of 2 also falls below the threshold of 5. A definitive negative result would need the whole interval below the threshold, both positive categories require rejection of the null hypothesis, and an interval that includes zero is not statistically significant.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid states that H0 is not rejected when the 95% CI for a mean difference includes 0. COMLEX: do not abandon a therapy on the strength of an inconclusive, underpowered trial.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — type II error and inadequate power","isHighYield":true}],"4":[{"id":"sdl04_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Bias (Systematic Error) vs. Chance (Random Error)","batch":1,"stem":"Which property distinguishes bias from chance as a source of error in a research study?","choices":{"A":"Bias affects results equally in both directions","B":"Bias shrinks toward zero as the sample size grows","C":"Bias distorts results consistently in one direction","D":"Bias arises from individual biological variation","E":"Bias can be estimated and reduced by statistical tests"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bias is a systematic deviation from the truth that tends to distort findings in a single direction, consistently overestimating or underestimating the true effect. Chance is random error that varies in both directions, arises from biological variation, sampling error, and measurement error, can be estimated mathematically, and tends to cancel out as the sample size grows. Bias is prevented by strict methodology and proper data analysis, not by enlarging the sample, and hypothesis tests quantify chance rather than bias.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid pairs systematic error with reduced accuracy, or validity, and random error with reduced precision, or reliability. COMLEX: a bigger study does not fix a biased design.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 1 — a valid measurement is free from bias and keeps random error as small as possible","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Bias (Systematic Error) vs. Chance (Random Error)","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following is one of the three major sources of chance, as opposed to bias, in research?","choices":{"A":"Selection bias","B":"Confounding","C":"Sampling error","D":"Measurement bias","E":"Observer-expectancy bias"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The three major sources of random error, or chance, are individual biological variation, sampling error, and measurement error; their influence can be estimated and reduced by design, for example with larger samples. Selection bias, confounding, measurement bias, and observer-expectancy bias are systematic errors that push results in one direction and are not reduced by a larger sample.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes that random error decreases precision, and that greater precision lowers the standard deviation and raises statistical power. COMLEX: repeating a blood pressure measurement reduces the effect of random fluctuation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 1 — a valid measurement is free from bias and keeps random error as small as possible","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q03","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Selection Bias, Measurement Bias, and Confounding Bias","batch":1,"stem":"Researchers studying eye irritation among factory workers exposed to formaldehyde survey all employees present at work on the study day. Workers with the most severe irritation are the most likely to be absent. Which type of bias does this introduce?","choices":{"A":"Measurement bias","B":"Recall bias","C":"Confounding bias","D":"Selection bias","E":"Lead-time bias"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Selection bias is a systematic difference between the individuals selected for a study and those who are not; here the outcome itself, severe eye irritation, makes affected workers unavailable, so they are omitted and the irritation caused by exposure is underestimated. Measurement bias is incorrect determination of exposure or outcome in those who are studied, confounding is distortion by a third factor, recall bias is altered recall of past exposure, and lead-time bias concerns earlier diagnosis by screening.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists attrition bias, in which participants lost to follow-up have a different prognosis, as a form of selection bias. COMLEX: occupational studies that sample only workers present at work can miss those most affected by an exposure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 2 — random sampling guards against the systematic bias of haphazard or convenient samples","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Selection Bias, Measurement Bias, and Confounding Bias","batch":1,"stem":"In a study of risk factors for upper gastrointestinal bleeding, a clinician questions patients admitted with bleeding in detail about analgesic use but asks just one brief question about it in patients admitted without bleeding. Which type of bias results?","choices":{"A":"Selection bias","B":"Measurement bias","C":"Lead-time bias","D":"Attrition bias","E":"Confounding"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Measurement bias, also called information, observation, or classification bias, results from incorrect determination of exposure, outcome, or both, such as searching for information unequally between groups; probing analgesic history more thoroughly in patients with bleeding inflates the apparent association. The groups were not selected differently, no third variable is distorting the association, there is no screening-related timing issue, and no participants were lost to follow-up.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid defines measurement bias as information gathered in a systematically distorted manner, reduced by objective, standardized data collection planned ahead of time. COMLEX: use the same structured history for every patient in a study.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 2 — random sampling guards against the systematic bias of haphazard or convenient samples","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q05","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — The Concept of a Confounding Variable","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following is one of the required conditions for a variable to be considered a potential confounder?","choices":{"A":"It must lie on the causal pathway to the outcome","B":"It must be a surrogate measure of the exposure","C":"It must arise from random error in measurement","D":"It must have no association with the exposure","E":"It must be a known risk factor for the outcome"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A potential confounder must be a known risk factor for the outcome, must be associated with the exposure, must not be an intermediate step in the causal pathway between exposure and outcome, and should not be a surrogate for the exposure. A variable on the causal pathway or serving as a surrogate is excluded by these conditions, a confounder must be associated with the exposure rather than unrelated to it, and confounding is a systematic distortion rather than random error.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid defines confounding as a factor related to both exposure and outcome, not on the causal pathway, that distorts the effect on the outcome. COMLEX: matching and regression analysis are used to control measured confounders.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 3 — confounding distorts the internal validity of an observed association","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Bias (Systematic Error) vs. Chance (Random Error)","batch":2,"stem":"In a validation study, a patient's true intra-arterial blood pressure measured with an invasive transducer catheter is 80 mmHg. Repeated sphygmomanometer readings in that patient are 84, 86, and 94 mmHg, and the average of ten such readings is 90 mmHg. Which best describes the 10 mmHg difference between the cuff average and the catheter value?","choices":{"A":"A systematic measurement bias of the indirect method","B":"Sampling error from studying a single patient","C":"Confounding by the patient's hypertension","D":"Biological variation in the patient's true pressure","E":"Random error that cancels out with more readings"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The catheter is the gold standard, and the constant 10 mmHg gap between it and the indirect cuff method is a measurement bias, a systematic error that more readings will not remove. The fluctuation of individual cuff readings around 90 mmHg is chance, and those random deviations cancel out as the number of readings increases so that the average approaches 90, not 80. No sample of patients or exposure-outcome association is involved, so sampling error and confounding do not apply.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid gives a faulty automatic sphygmomanometer as its example of measurement bias, and links systematic error with reduced accuracy. COMLEX: calibrate devices against a reference standard rather than relying on repeated readings.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — reliability versus validity of a measurement","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q07","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Selection Bias, Measurement Bias, and Confounding Bias","batch":2,"stem":"A surgeon reviews hospital records to compare a new laparoscopic procedure with open surgery for colon cancer. Over the review period, the laparoscopic procedure was offered mainly to younger patients with less advanced disease, while older patients with more extensive tumors had open surgery. Five-year survival is much higher in the laparoscopic group. Which type of bias most threatens this comparison?","choices":{"A":"Measurement bias","B":"Selection bias","C":"Recall bias","D":"Random error","E":"Lead-time bias"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In clinical comparisons, selection bias arises when groups differ in key determinants of outcome other than the intervention, such as age and disease severity; the healthier laparoscopic group would be expected to survive longer regardless of the procedure. Nothing suggests survival was recorded differently between groups, patient recall is not involved, random error would not produce a consistent advantage for one group, and lead-time bias concerns earlier diagnosis by screening rather than treatment allocation. This is also called confounding by indication.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid defines selection bias as nonrandom sampling or treatment allocation and lists randomization as the strategy that creates comparable groups. COMLEX: nonrandomized comparisons of surgical techniques are prone to unequal case mix.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — random selection and comparable, representative groups","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q08","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Selection Bias, Measurement Bias, and Confounding Bias","batch":2,"stem":"A case-control study finds that patients with lung cancer were more likely than controls to drink several cups of coffee a day. On further review, coffee drinkers in the study were far more likely to smoke cigarettes than non-drinkers, and smoking is a well-established cause of lung cancer. Which type of bias best explains the apparent link between coffee and lung cancer?","choices":{"A":"Attrition bias","B":"Selection bias","C":"Measurement bias","D":"Lead-time bias","E":"Confounding bias"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Confounding bias occurs when the relationship between an exposure and an outcome is mixed with the effect of a third factor; smoking is a risk factor for lung cancer and is associated with coffee drinking, so it creates a spurious association between coffee and lung cancer. Nothing indicates that cases and controls were selected unequally or that coffee intake was recorded differently between groups, and lead-time and attrition bias do not apply to this design.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid uses coffee and lung cancer, with smoking as the true cause, as its example of confounding, which is reduced by crossover designs, matching, and regression analysis. COMLEX: always ask what else differs between exposed and unexposed patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — internal validity and freedom from distortion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — The Concept of a Confounding Variable","batch":2,"stem":"Early observational studies reported that women taking oral contraceptives had a markedly higher risk of myocardial infarction. Later analysis showed that a large proportion of the women who used oral contraceptives in these cohorts also smoked cigarettes, and much of the apparent association was attributable to smoking. Why did smoking qualify as a confounder in these studies?","choices":{"A":"It lay on the causal pathway from pill use to MI","B":"It served as a surrogate marker for pill use itself","C":"It was an independent MI risk factor linked to pill use","D":"It lowered MI risk but was common among pill users","E":"It reduced the precision but not the accuracy of results"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Smoking satisfied the conditions of a confounder: it is a major independent risk factor for myocardial infarction, and it was strongly associated with oral contraceptive use in the cohort, so the studies were actually measuring the mixed-in effect of smoking. A variable on the causal pathway or acting as a surrogate for the exposure would not qualify as a confounder, smoking raises rather than lowers the risk of myocardial infarction, and confounding is a systematic error that reduces accuracy rather than a random error affecting precision. Note that First Aid uses oral contraceptives and smoking with DVT as an example of effect modification, a different concept.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid uses oral contraceptives, smoking, and DVT as its example of effect modification, in which a true association differs across strata, and contrasts it with confounding, in which the association disappears after stratification. COMLEX: stratify by smoking when evaluating cardiovascular risks of hormonal therapy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — confounding bias as one of the three major categories of bias","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q10","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — The Concept of a Confounding Variable","batch":2,"stem":"A cohort study examines whether obesity increases the risk of myocardial infarction. A co-investigator proposes adjusting for hypertension as a confounder. Another investigator objects, noting that obesity raises blood pressure and that the elevated blood pressure in turn raises the risk of myocardial infarction. Why should hypertension not be treated as a confounder in this analysis?","choices":{"A":"It is not a known risk factor for myocardial infarction","B":"It is an intermediate step between exposure and outcome","C":"It is not associated with obesity in most populations","D":"It is a surrogate measure of the patient's age","E":"It distorts results randomly in both directions"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A potential confounder must not be an intermediate step in the causal pathway between exposure and outcome; when obesity raises blood pressure and blood pressure raises infarction risk, hypertension carries part of the effect of obesity, and adjusting for it would remove the portion of that effect acting through blood pressure. Hypertension is a known risk factor for myocardial infarction and is associated with obesity, it is not a surrogate for age, and confounding is a systematic rather than a random distortion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid defines a confounder as related to both exposure and outcome but not on a causal pathway. COMLEX: decide which variables are mediators before choosing what to adjust for in a regression model.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — the four conditions a confounder must satisfy","isHighYield":true}],"5":[{"id":"sdl05_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Definitions of Reliability and Validity in Research","batch":1,"stem":"The validity of a measurement tool is best defined as the degree to which the tool does which of the following?","choices":{"A":"Gives the same result on repeated testing","B":"Measures what it purports to measure","C":"Applies to populations outside the study","D":"Detects a difference when one truly exists","E":"Can be repeated by different observers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Validity is the degree to which a measurement tool or test measures what it purports to measure; a valid measurement corresponds to the truth, being free from bias with random error kept small. Giving the same result on repeated testing and reproducibility across observers describe reliability, applicability to other populations is external validity of a study, and detecting a true difference is statistical power.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid equates validity with accuracy, the closeness of results to the true values and the absence of systematic error. COMLEX: a screening questionnaire must be checked against a reference standard before it is trusted.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 — bias is systematic error and chance is random error","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Definitions of Reliability and Validity in Research","batch":1,"stem":"Reliability in research measurement is also referred to as which of the following?","choices":{"A":"Accuracy","B":"Reproducibility","C":"Generalizability","D":"Internal validity","E":"Statistical power"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Reliability is also called reproducibility: repeated measurements of a stable subject by different observers or instruments, at different times and locations, give similar, consistent results. Accuracy is the closeness of a measurement to the true value and corresponds to validity, generalizability is external validity, internal validity concerns whether a study's results are correct for its own sample, and power is the probability of detecting a true effect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid equates reliability with precision, the consistency and reproducibility of a test and the absence of random variation. COMLEX: inter-rater reliability matters for palpatory findings that different examiners must reproduce.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 — bias is systematic error and chance is random error","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q03","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguishing Between Reliability and Validity","batch":1,"stem":"A clinic scale reads 72.0 kg every time a patient steps on it during a single visit. The patient's weight on a calibrated reference scale is 68.0 kg. How is the clinic scale best described?","choices":{"A":"Valid but not generalizable","B":"Valid but not reliable","C":"Both reliable and valid","D":"Neither reliable nor valid","E":"Reliable but not valid"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The clinic scale gives the same value on every reading, so it is reliable, but that value is 4 kg away from the true weight because of a systematic error, so it is not valid; high reliability does not ensure validity. It cannot be called valid because its readings do not match the truth, and it cannot be called unreliable because its readings are perfectly consistent. Generalizability describes study findings rather than a single instrument.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid shows precision and accuracy as independent axes, with systematic error reducing accuracy and random error reducing precision. COMLEX: recalibrate equipment that is consistently off rather than taking more readings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 — a constant offset from the gold standard is bias, and scatter around the average is chance","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguishing Between Reliability and Validity","batch":1,"stem":"Which statement about reliability and validity is correct?","choices":{"A":"A reliable measure is by definition also valid","B":"Validity describes consistency across repeated readings","C":"Reliability describes closeness to the true value","D":"High reliability does not ensure a valid measure","E":"Systematic error lowers reliability but not validity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A tool can be highly reliable, consistently producing the same value, yet invalid if that value is far from the true target because of systematic error or bias. The two are interdependent, but consistency alone does not establish accuracy. Validity relates to accuracy, getting the true value, and reliability relates to consistency, getting the same result repeatedly; systematic error mainly reduces validity, whereas random error mainly reduces reliability.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid equates precision with reliability and accuracy with validity, so a test can be precise yet inaccurate. COMLEX: consistent findings are not necessarily correct findings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 — a constant offset from the gold standard is bias, and scatter around the average is chance","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q05","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Internal Validity vs. External Validity","batch":1,"stem":"What does external validity of a research study refer to?","choices":{"A":"Freedom of results from bias within the studied sample","B":"Consistency of a measure across repeated observations","C":"Applying findings to other settings and populations","D":"Agreement of a measure with an invasive gold standard","E":"Absence of random error from the measurements taken"},"correct":"C","explanation":"External validity, or generalizability, is the degree to which a study's findings apply to other settings, broader populations, or daily clinical practice. Freedom from bias and chance within the studied sample is internal validity, consistency across repeated observations is reliability, agreement with a gold standard is measurement validity, and absence of random error relates to reliability and precision.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes that meta-analysis improves power, strength of evidence, and generalizability, or external validity. COMLEX: before applying a trial result, ask whether its participants resemble your patient.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 2 — a representative random sample supports applying findings to the target population","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Definitions of Reliability and Validity in Research","batch":2,"stem":"A new 10-item depression questionnaire gives nearly identical scores when the same stable patients complete it two weeks apart. However, its scores agree poorly with diagnoses made by structured psychiatric interview, which serves as the reference standard. What does the poor agreement with the interview call into question?","choices":{"A":"Whether the tool measures what it claims to measure","B":"Whether the tool gives consistent repeated scores","C":"Whether different raters obtain similar scores","D":"Whether the results will apply to other clinic populations","E":"Whether the sample size gives adequate power"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Validity is the degree to which a tool measures what it purports to measure, and poor agreement with the reference diagnosis questions the questionnaire's validity. The stable repeat scores show that the tool is reliable, so consistency is not in doubt, and agreement between raters is another aspect of reliability. Applicability to other clinic populations is external validity of a study, and power concerns detecting a true difference in a trial.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid defines accuracy, or validity, as the closeness of test results to the true values. COMLEX: a reliable screening tool can still misclassify patients if it is not valid.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — bias as systematic error","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q07","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguishing Between Reliability and Validity","batch":2,"stem":"A new handheld thermometer is tested on a patient whose core temperature, measured with a reference probe, is 37.0 degrees C. Five successive readings with the handheld device are 36.2, 37.9, 36.5, 37.6, and 36.8 degrees C, and the mean of the five readings is 37.0 degrees C. Which combination best describes the handheld thermometer?","choices":{"A":"Low precision with high accuracy","B":"High precision with low accuracy","C":"High precision with high accuracy","D":"Low precision with low accuracy","E":"High internal and high external validity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The readings are widely spread, so precision (reliability) is low, yet their mean equals the true value, so accuracy (validity) of the averaged result is high; First Aid calls this high accuracy with low precision. High precision would require tightly clustered readings, low accuracy would require the mean to sit away from 37.0 degrees C because of a systematic error, and internal and external validity describe research studies rather than a single instrument. Any single reading from this device cannot be trusted, but the average of several readings can.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid links random error with reduced precision, or reliability, and systematic error with reduced accuracy, or validity. COMLEX: average several readings from an imprecise but unbiased device.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — chance varies in both directions and cancels out with more readings","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Internal Validity vs. External Validity","batch":2,"stem":"A well-conducted randomized controlled trial at an academic center shows that a new drug reduces hospitalizations in patients aged 40 to 60 years who have no other chronic illnesses. A community physician is considering the drug for an 82-year-old patient with chronic kidney disease and heart failure. Which statement best describes the trial in relation to this patient?","choices":{"A":"External validity is high because internal validity is high","B":"Internal validity is likely compromised by the restricted sample","C":"Internal validity may be high but external validity limited","D":"The findings lack both internal and external validity","E":"External validity ensures the result applies to the patient"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Internal validity is a prerequisite for external validity but does not guarantee it: a trial can be valid for its restricted sample yet fail to generalize when its participants do not reflect other populations, such as older patients with multiple comorbidities. A restricted sample does not by itself lower internal validity, nothing suggests bias within the trial, and high internal validity does not ensure that results apply to a patient unlike those studied.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes that meta-analysis improves generalizability, or external validity, of study findings. COMLEX: older patients with multimorbidity are often under-represented in drug trials.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — representative samples and inference to the target population","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Internal Validity vs. External Validity","batch":2,"stem":"A cohort study of a new asthma inhaler recorded exacerbations far more thoroughly in the comparison group than in the inhaler group, so its estimate of benefit is distorted. A journal reviewer comments that the study's findings therefore cannot be generalized to any other population. Which principle supports the reviewer's comment?","choices":{"A":"External validity can offset poor internal validity","B":"Generalizability is determined by sample size","C":"Measurement bias affects reliability but not validity","D":"Internal validity is a prerequisite for external validity","E":"Internal validity guarantees external validity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A study must possess internal validity to be of scientific use, and internal validity is a mandatory prerequisite for external validity; when unequal outcome recording distorts results within the sample, they cannot be validly extrapolated elsewhere. External validity cannot rescue a biased study, a large sample does not remove bias, measurement bias is a systematic error that undermines validity, and internal validity is necessary but not sufficient for external validity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid defines measurement bias as information gathered in a systematically distorted manner. COMLEX: appraise a study's internal validity before asking whether it applies to your patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — measurement bias from unequal determination of outcomes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q10","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Internal Validity vs. External Validity","batch":2,"stem":"Investigators planning a trial of a new physical therapy protocol after knee replacement want to be confident that any difference they observe between their two groups of enrolled patients is genuine and not distorted by bias or chance. Which approach most directly serves this goal?","choices":{"A":"Enrolling a sample that resembles community practice","B":"Recruiting patients from many countries and settings","C":"Meticulous design and data collection with sound analysis","D":"Choosing outcomes that matter to patients in daily life","E":"Reporting results to clinicians in many settings"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The goal described is internal validity, whether the observed effects within the study sample are genuine and free from bias and chance, and achieving it requires meticulous study design, careful data collection, and appropriate analysis. Enrolling a representative sample, recruiting from many countries and settings, and choosing patient-relevant outcomes mainly improve external validity or relevance, and dissemination of results does not affect validity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists randomization, blinding, placebo use, and standardized data collection among the strategies that reduce bias. COMLEX: a trial must first be internally valid before its results can guide rehabilitation practice.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — selection, measurement, and confounding bias","isHighYield":true}]};
